      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate long-term patterns of luminal changes after implantation of different types of drug-eluting stents (DES), we analyzed the serial angiographic outcomes of patients implanted with zotarolimus-eluting stents (ZES), sirolimus-eluting stents (SES), or paclitaxel-eluting stents (PES)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Little is known regarding long-term luminal changes after DES implantation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "As a subgroup analysis of the ZEST trial, we performed complete angiographic evaluation immediately after the procedure and at 9 months and 2 years in 111 patients with 165 lesions (36 patients with ZES, 40 with SES, and 35 with PES)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Baseline clinical, angiographic, and procedural characteristics were similar among the three groups. Quantitative angiographic analysis revealed significant decreases in minimal luminal diameter 9 months after stent implantation in the ZES (from 2.71 ± 0.49 to 2.21 ± 0.42 mm, P < 0.001), SES (from 2.79 ± 0.49 to 2.58 ± 0.57 mm, P < 0.001), and PES (from 2.66 ± 0.45 to 2.19 ± 0.52 mm, P < 0.001) groups. However, significant late improvements with different degree in luminal diameter were observed between 9 months and 2 years in the ZES (from 2.21 ± 0.42 to 2.39 ± 0.58 mm, P = 0.001), SES (from 2.58 ± 0.57 to 2.66 ± 0.60 mm, P = 0.039), and PES (from 2.19 ± 0.52 to 2.43 ± 0.52 mm, P < 0.001) groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Serial angiographic follow-up study revealed a biphasic luminal response after DES implantation, characterized by an early progression phase for the first 9 months and a late regression phase from 9 months to 2 years."
          }
        ]
      },
